        {
          "id": "2020-ml-speech-masterclass",
          "year": 2020,
          "title": "Machine Learning and Speech",
          "authors": ["Carpuat M.", "Natarajan P."],
          "venue": "Digital Data Flows Masterclass",
          "location": "Brussels",
          "publisher": "Brussels Privacy Hub, Vrije Universiteit Brussels & Future of Privacy Forum",
          "category": "professional learning",
          "mediaType": "video",
          "contentType": "training",
          "displayContext": "modal",
          "summary": "Rob van Eijk of the Future of Privacy Forum hosts a Digital Data Flows Masterclass on machine learning and speech, with Sara Jordan and Brenda Leong. Marine Carpuat of the University of Maryland explains neural machine translation and why semi-supervised techniques such as back-translation matter when paired training data is scarce. Her research focuses on \"fluently inadequate\" translations. These errors are rare but look plausible, as when a system translated a Facebook post as \"attack them\" and the police arrested its author. In the Q&A she admits that the research community has not seriously addressed removing data from trained models under rules such as the GDPR. Prem Natarajan of Amazon Alexa AI describes how conversational AI works. He presents semi-supervised learning, active learning, transfer learning, and self-learning as a path to less labelled data and less human review of recordings. Carpuat closes with the point that personalisation will make privacy questions more pressing.",
          "metadata": {
            "moderators": "Eijk, R. & Jordan S."
          },
          "links": [
            {
              "type": "video",
              "url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=uRJsTy50Sqs",
              "title": "Watch recording",
              "status": "active",
              "lastChecked": "2026-10-25T11:34:12+01:00",
              "archiveUrls": []
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "2020-iab-adtech-part1",
          "year": 2020,
          "title": "Inzicht in Adtech PART 1",
          "summary": "Rob van Eijk gives the first part of an IAB Nederland webinar on understanding adtech, moderated by Tim Geenen of LiveRamp, IAB Nederland board member for privacy. Van Eijk explains the browser's components, rendering engines, and storage options. He takes apart two display ads to show the images, scripts, and third-party calls behind them. He maps the parties in real-time bidding, the identifiers in bid requests, and his measurements of third-party connections on newspaper sites across Europe. He shows browser fingerprinting with canvas, fonts, and audio, and how the default protections in Chrome, Firefox, and Safari differ. In the Q&A he covers cookie audits, including social sharing widgets that sites wrongly label as functional, tracking in apps, the Dutch exemption for low-impact analytics cookies, and the consent requirements of Planet49. Time ran out before the planned walkthrough of consent banners, which became the second session.",
          "authors": ["Eijk R. van", "Geenen T."],
