        {
          "id": "2019-online-advertising-technologies",
          "year": 2019,
          "title": "Online Advertising Technologies",
          "authors": ["Eijk R. van", "Towvim A."],
          "venue": "Digital Data Flows Masterclass",
          "location": "Brussels",
          "publisher": "Brussels Privacy Hub, Vrije Universiteit Brussels & Future of Privacy Forum",
          "category": "professional learning",
          "mediaType": "video",
          "contentType": "training",
          "displayContext": "modal",
          "summary": "Stacey Gray of the Future of Privacy Forum moderates a Digital Data Flows Masterclass on online advertising technologies. Adam Towvim of Brandeis University's business school traces the move from direct media buying to programmatic advertising. He explains how advertisers, publishers, and intermediaries use cookies, pixels, hashed emails, and cross-device linking, and answers questions on ad fraud. Rob van Eijk of Leiden University walks through the nine steps of real-time bidding, from the publisher's ad slot to the bid requests that go out to hundreds of partners, including deal IDs and header bidding. He presents his graph-based research that separates real-time bidding networks on European news sites. In the open Q&A the speakers discuss why notice and consent is hard when nobody knows the bidders in advance, and how identity resolution links identifiers across devices.",
